{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"d11d5763cd810485","name":"card_unit_kings_own_scottish","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Britain/BrothersInArms/units/card_unit_kings_own_scottish","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"KING'S OWN SCOTTISH","text":"Pincer: Damage dealt to this unit is dealt to its Pincer partner instead.","flavorText":"The King’s Own Scottish Borderers (KOSBs) was an infantry regiment of the British Army that was first raised in 1689 and stayed active until 2006 when it was merged with the Royal Scots Battalion to form the Royal Scots Borderers. Between the wars the battalions of the regiment were in service all over the British Empire, but were called back home when WWII started in 1939. The 1st Battalion was sent to France to serve in the British Expeditionary Force as part of the 9th Brigade of the 3rd Infantry Division and took part in the Dunkirk evacuation in June 1940. The 1st and the 6th Battalion would later serve in the Normandy landings in 1944 and the following Battle for Caen. The 2nd Battalion was sent to Burma as part of the 89th Brigade of the 7th Indian Infantry Division and participated in the Battle of the Admin Box and the Battle of Imphal in the Burma Campaign.  The 4th and 5th Battalion were sent to Saint-Malo in France as part of the 155th Brigade of the 52nd (Lowland) Division to service in the British Expeditionary Force, and were evacuated from Cherbourg in 1940. In 1944 they took part in the Operation Infatuate which successfully cleared the seaway to Antwerp port for the Allied forces, and in 1945 they took part in capturing the city Bremen.  The 7th Battalion participated in the failed Operation Market Garden as part of the 1st Airlanding Brigade of 1st Airborne Division, where the battalion suffered 90% casualties during the Battle of Arnhem while defending the perimeter in Oosterbeek against the 2nd SS Panzer Corps.","faction":"Britain","type":"infantry","rarity":"Common","cost":2,"operationCost":1,"attack":1,"defense":5},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_unit_kings_own_scottish","title":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_kings_own_scottish_title","SourceString":"KING'S OWN SCOTTISH","LocalizedString":"KING'S OWN SCOTTISH"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::infantry","attack":1,"defense":5,"range":1,"faction":"EFactionEnum::Britain","kredits":2,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_kings_own_scottish_text","SourceString":"Pincer: Damage dealt to this unit is dealt to its Pincer partner instead.","LocalizedString":"Pincer: Damage dealt to this unit is dealt to its Pincer partner instead."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_kings_own_scottish_history","SourceString":"The King’s Own Scottish Borderers (KOSBs) was an infantry regiment of the British Army that was first raised in 1689 and stayed active until 2006 when it was merged with the Royal Scots Battalion to form the Royal Scots Borderers. Between the wars the battalions of the regiment were in service all over the British Empire, but were called back home when WWII started in 1939. The 1st Battalion was sent to France to serve in the British Expeditionary Force as part of the 9th Brigade of the 3rd Infantry Division and took part in the Dunkirk evacuation in June 1940. The 1st and the 6th Battalion would later serve in the Normandy landings in 1944 and the following Battle for Caen. The 2nd Battalion was sent to Burma as part of the 89th Brigade of the 7th Indian Infantry Division and participated in the Battle of the Admin Box and the Battle of Imphal in the Burma Campaign.  The 4th and 5th Battalion were sent to Saint-Malo in France as part of the 155th Brigade of the 52nd (Lowland) Division to service in the British Expeditionary Force, and were evacuated from Cherbourg in 1940. In 1944 they took part in the Operation Infatuate which successfully cleared the seaway to Antwerp port for the Allied forces, and in 1945 they took part in capturing the city Bremen.  The 7th Battalion participated in the failed Operation Market Garden as part of the 1st Airlanding Brigade of 1st Airborne Division, where the battalion suffered 90% casualties during the Battle of Arnhem while defending the perimeter in Oosterbeek against the 2nd SS Panzer Corps.","LocalizedString":"The King’s Own Scottish Borderers (KOSBs) was an infantry regiment of the British Army that was first raised in 1689 and stayed active until 2006 when it was merged with the Royal Scots Battalion to form the Royal Scots Borderers. Between the wars the battalions of the regiment were in service all over the British Empire, but were called back home when WWII started in 1939. The 1st Battalion was sent to France to serve in the British Expeditionary Force as part of the 9th Brigade of the 3rd Infantry Division and took part in the Dunkirk evacuation in June 1940. The 1st and the 6th Battalion would later serve in the Normandy landings in 1944 and the following Battle for Caen. The 2nd Battalion was sent to Burma as part of the 89th Brigade of the 7th Indian Infantry Division and participated in the Battle of the Admin Box and the Battle of Imphal in the Burma Campaign.  The 4th and 5th Battalion were sent to Saint-Malo in France as part of the 155th Brigade of the 52nd (Lowland) Division to service in the British Expeditionary Force, and were evacuated from Cherbourg in 1940. In 1944 they took part in the Operation Infatuate which successfully cleared the seaway to Antwerp port for the Allied forces, and in 1945 they took part in capturing the city Bremen.  The 7th Battalion participated in the failed Operation Market Garden as part of the 1st Airlanding Brigade of 1st Airborne Division, where the battalion suffered 90% casualties during the Battle of Arnhem while defending the perimeter in Oosterbeek against the 2nd SS Panzer Corps."},"operationCost":1,"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Common","selectTargetOnPlayedFromHand":true,"cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::BrothersInArms","isR